a number called gen_envs is 0.

(How many flag schemas this program declared. Kept because loop_gen and
 the tests report on it; it is no longer a NAME - names come off the
 manifest (gen_manifest.vox), which is what let the flag block stop
 being four identical lines in every program.)

a number called gen_flags is 0.

a boolean called gen_uses_flag_reader is true.

(Plan 327 Part A: the argv the harness will hand this program, and what
 the program should therefore see. The generator CHOOSES the arguments,
 so for once it knows the expected values at generation time — which is
 the deferred output-oracle problem already solved in one corner. A
 program given a valid invocation asserts its flags parsed to exactly
 what was passed and Exits with a distinct code if not, so a wrong
 answer becomes a detectable one without any reference implementation.

 gen_argv is read by loop_gen and handed to BOTH of the oracle's runs.
 If the two runs ever got different argv, the difference between them
 would BE the input and every program would be a false nondeterminism
 finding.

 gen_asserts_argv is false for the hostile shapes, where the point is
 that the program must not crash rather than that it must agree with a
 prediction — a malformed number raises and yields 0, a missing value
 leaves the flag empty, and an unknown flag is not consumed at all.
 Asserting on those would be asserting on behaviour the fuzzer is
 supposed to be QUESTIONING.)

a list called gen_argv is [].

(Which flags this program's argv actually supplies, and what each
 should therefore hold. A manifest may hold no text flag, or no number
 flag, or none at all, so each is a pair: whether there is one, and what
 it should be.)

a number called gen_text_flag is 0.

a text called gen_expected_label is "".

a number called gen_number_flag is 0.

a text called gen_expected_count is "0".

a number called gen_boolean_flag is 0.

a boolean called gen_expects_on is false.

a boolean called gen_asserts_argv is false.

a number called gen_reader_calls is 0.

(`environment's`: bug #24 (an unset variable segfaulted, uncatchable by
 On error) and bug #26 (out-of-range positional properties - first/last
 on an empty environment) - both fixed in 0.4.3/0.4.4 per CHANGELOG, so
 both are now defined-behaviour-under-test, the same standing the
 buffer/list/map oob leaves already have.

 The inrange leaf reads real environment state - count, a named lookup
 of PATH - but two fixed regressions mean it must never print the raw
 result, and must never reuse a fixed local name. An earlier version
 printed environment's count and environment's "PATH" directly, and
 one landed in a checked-in test fixture verbatim: home directory, tool
 versions, every installed plugin, on a public repo. Reading is exactly
 the coverage bugs #24 and #26 are about; printing what was read is not
 required to exercise that and is the part that leaked. So every read
 here is immediately collapsed to a boolean before it is printed -
 `as a boolean` (0 is false, nonzero is true) for the count, and a
 buffer's own `empty` property (declaring the buffer directly from
 environment's "PATH", the same construction 'needle buf' elsewhere in
 this file uses to turn a text expression into a buffer) for the named
 lookup, never the text itself. `environment's empty` is already a
 boolean and was never the problem.

 The second regression a fixed local name caused: this leaf is reachable
 both as a nested block's mandatory closer (inside a conditionally-taken
 If branch) and as an ordinary top-level statement, in the same program,
 and a fixed name (`envpathbuf`) collided with itself across the two.
 Hand-reduced to a compiler defect with nothing environment-specific
 left in it: `If false-condition then, a buffer called b is "x".` /
 `a buffer called b is "y".` segfaults even though the first
 declaration's branch never runs - a text/buffer declared on an untaken
 If path corrupts a later, same-named declaration elsewhere. This reads
 like bug #25's family (a declaration on a conditional path leaving bad
 storage behind) but #25's own fix, read literally, only names On
 error/While/for-each/Repeat bodies - not If. Reported separately as a
 finding, not tuned around; the generator-side fix is what every other
 leaf in this file already does and this one alone was not doing -
 counter-suffixed names (env{gen_envs}count, env{gen_envs}pathbuf), so
 two calls can never collide regardless of which one lands inside a
 branch that turns out not to run.

 All five lines are plain reads/declarations with no error path, so this
 stays a safe closer.)

To 'gen leaf environment inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_envs to gen_envs add 1.
    a text called 'count decl' is "a number called env{gen_envs}count is environment's count".
    a text called 'count check' is "Print env{gen_envs}count as a boolean".
    a text called 'empty line' is "Print environment's empty".
    a text called 'path decl' is "a buffer called env{gen_envs}pathbuf is environment's \"PATH\"".
    a text called 'path check' is "Print env{gen_envs}pathbuf's empty".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['count decl', 'count check', 'empty line', 'path decl', 'path check'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(The oob leaf's own local, 'the missing value', is a fixed quoted name
 rather than a counter-suffixed one - safe unlike the inrange leaf's
 former envpathbuf, because this leaf is unsafe-closer/remap-only (see
 below), which this file's own avoid-onerror machinery guarantees means
 its real body only ever renders at the true top level, never inside a
 conditionally-taken branch - there is no second scope for the same
 name to collide with. Counter-suffixing it anyway would cost nothing
 and remove the need to reason about that guarantee holding forever, so
 it is done here too, purely as defensive consistency with the fix
 above rather than because this leaf reproduces the defect.)

To 'gen leaf environment oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_envs to gen_envs add 1.
    a number called 'probe id' is 'rng below' of 1000000.
    a text called decl is "a text called 'the missing value {gen_envs}' is environment's \"VOXFUZZ_UNSET_{'probe id'}\"".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"environment variable not set\"".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print 'the missing value {gen_envs}'".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, handler, 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(`arguments's`: bugs #23 and #26 lived here, both fixed. The generated
 program runs with no user arguments (runner.vox's 'run program' always
 passes an empty argument list), so count/empty are the properties that
 stay meaningful either way - count is always 1, empty is always true,
 and both are plain reads with no error path, so this is a safe closer.

 `arguments's name` was dropped here as a direct consequence of D1
 (per-run scratch isolation, see loop_gen.vox / sandbox.vox): the
 generated program's own path is now `./vf_scratch/run_{pid}_{seed}/
 vf_gen_bin`, and `arguments's name` returns that path. The pid varies
 per run, so printing it would make a generated program's stdout
 non-deterministic, and tests/060_loop_gen.expected + tests/070_cli.expected
 pin program output by exact diff - every run would break the gate. This
 is the same shape `gen leaf environment inrange` already solved for
 `environment's "PATH"`: raw host/run state is non-deterministic, so the
 leaf reads only the deterministic properties (count, empty) and never
 prints the raw text. count and empty stay because both are fixed
 regardless of the path - count is always 1, empty always true with no
 user args.

 `first`/`second`/`last`/`the argument at` would, with zero arguments
 passed, ALWAYS hit their out-of-range path rather than sometimes
 exercising the in-range one - the brief's own instruction is to flag
 that rather than emit something that can only ever prove one thing;
 noted in the task report instead. `arguments's all` still gets
 exercised for real, just via 'gen leaf treating print' above, since a
 treating clause needs a collection anyway and an always-empty one is
 exactly the collection arguments's all always is - one fewer construct
 duplicated as its own leaf.)

To 'gen leaf arguments inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'count line' is "Print arguments's count".
    a text called 'empty line' is "Print arguments's empty".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['count line', 'empty line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Casts, `absolute`, and `Break` - all three grep-confirmed absent from
 this generator before 2026-08-20, and `Break` was measured at 0 of 40
 programs, the only construct at literally zero.

 The cast pair is the point: `n as text` followed by `t as a number`
 round-trips a value through text and back, and it is fed an extreme
 literal, so i64's own limits go through the conversion. That is how a
 saturating or wrapping cast would show itself - and compiler bug #34
 (a float outside +/-2^63 formats as 9223372036854775808.372...) is
 exactly a conversion boundary, found by hand while writing these
 emitters rather than by the fuzzer, which could not reach it.

 `Break`'s loop is bounded by construction - a fixed range with a
 counter guard - so the program still always terminates. The guard's
 period closes only the If (LANGUAGE.md rule 1), so the loop body
 continues to the comma-joined line after it, which is why the print
 sits inside the loop rather than after it.)

To 'gen leaf cast and break' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a text called 'the literal' is 'gen extreme number'.
    a number called 'the ceiling' is 'rng below' of 20 add 5.
    a text called 'source decl' is "a number called cn{n} is {'the literal'}".
    a text called 'to text' is "a text called ct{n} is cn{n} as text".
    a text called 'print text' is "Print ct{n}".
    a text called 'round trip' is "a number called cb{n} is ct{n} as a number".
    a text called 'print round trip' is "Print cb{n}".
    a text called 'absolute probe' is "Print cn{n}'s absolute".
    a text called 'loop line' is "For each ce{n} from 1 to {'the ceiling'},".
    a text called 'the accumulate line' is "    a number called cs{n} is ce{n} multiply 2,".
    a text called 'the guard line' is "    If cs{n} is greater than {'the ceiling'} then, Break.".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['source decl', 'to text', 'print text', 'round trip', 'print round trip', 'absolute probe'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}{'loop line'}\n{indent}{'the accumulate line'}\n{indent}{'the guard line'}\n".

(Timers and time values — plan 323 listed these Tier CONFINE on the
 assumption that anything time-related needs a bound. A timer does not:
 `Start`/`Stop` around a loop that is already bounded by construction
 adds no waiting at all, and reading `elapsed` is a plain read. `Wait`
 IS the construct that needs a bound and is deliberately still not
 emitted here.

 Every assertion is a knowable one — elapsed cannot be negative, a unix
 timestamp cannot be non-positive, the year cannot be below 2000 — so
 this leaf prints a verdict rather than a raw number. That keeps the
 output deterministic despite the clock being anything but, which
 matters because the same seed must produce the same PROGRAM and this
 one's output must not wander between runs.

 Syntax verified by hand first: `the clock's elapsed in milliseconds`
 (the `in` cast only works on a timer's duration/elapsed), and
 `Get current time into now` rather than any possessive on a bare
 `time` — two forms I got wrong before reading LANGUAGE.md properly.)

To 'gen leaf timer and clock' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a number called 'the ceiling' is 'rng below' of 40 add 10.
    a text called 'timer decl' is "a timer called tk{n}".
    a text called 'timer start' is "Start tk{n}".
    a text called 'accumulator' is "a number called ta{n} is 0".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['timer decl', 'timer start', 'accumulator'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}For each te{n} from 1 to {'the ceiling'},\n".
    'gen emit' of "{indent}    Set ta{n} to ta{n} add te{n}.\n".
    a text called 'timer stop' is "Stop tk{n}".
    a text called 'print acc' is "Print ta{n}".
    a text called 'read elapsed' is "a number called tl{n} is the tk{n}'s elapsed in milliseconds".
    a text called 'check elapsed' is "If tl{n} is negative then, Print \"tl{n} NEGATIVE\". Otherwise, Print \"tl{n} ok\"".
    a text called 'get time' is "Get current time into tn{n}".
    a text called 'read unix' is "a number called tu{n} is the tn{n}'s unix".
    a text called 'check unix' is "If tu{n} is positive then, Print \"tu{n} ok\". Otherwise, Print \"tu{n} BAD\"".
    a text called 'read year' is "a number called ty{n} is the tn{n}'s year".
    a text called 'check year' is "If ty{n} is greater than 2000 then, Print \"ty{n} ok\". Otherwise, Print \"ty{n} BAD\"".
    a list called 'the tail lines' is ['timer stop', 'print acc', 'read elapsed', 'check elapsed', 'get time', 'read unix', 'check unix', 'read year', 'check year'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the tail lines'.

(Weighted so most programs get a sane invocation — the common path, and
 the one that has never run — while a minority get something hostile.
 The hostile shapes are drawn from behaviour probed by hand against
 0.4.7 and recorded in defect 10, so none of them is a guess.)

To 'gen build argv'.
    (A list is emptied by assignment; `clear` is buffer-only.)
    Set gen_argv to [].
    Set gen_expected_label to "".
    Set gen_expected_count to "0".
    Set gen_expects_on to false.
    Set gen_asserts_argv to false.
    (Which flags there are to play with. The old builder wrote "-a",
     "-b" and "-c" as literals because the schema was a fixed template;
     now each is whatever this program's manifest drew, and a program
     that drew no text flag simply has no label to pass or assert.)
    Set gen_text_flag to 0.
    Set gen_number_flag to 0.
    Set gen_boolean_flag to 0.
    a boolean called 'a text flag exists' is 'gen manifest pick a flag of kind' of 0.
    If 'a text flag exists' then,
        Set gen_text_flag to gen_pick_flag.
    a boolean called 'a number flag exists' is 'gen manifest pick a flag of kind' of 1.
    If 'a number flag exists' then,
        Set gen_number_flag to gen_pick_flag.
    a boolean called 'a boolean flag exists' is 'gen manifest pick a flag of kind' of 2.
    If 'a boolean flag exists' then,
        Set gen_boolean_flag to gen_pick_flag.
    a number called shape is 'rng below' of 10.
    (0-5: a valid invocation, asserted. Long form and short form are
     different code paths through one schema, so both are drawn.)
    If shape is less than 6 then,
        'gen build a valid argv',
        Return a number, 0.
    (6: a value of the wrong type for a number flag. Yields 0 AND raises,
     so a program can tell it from a real 0 - verified, defect 10.)
    If shape is 6 then,
        'gen pass a bad value to' of gen_number_flag and "notanumber",
        Return a number, 0.
    (7: a value past i64. Raises too - compiler bug #35's fix reaching
     the flag path.)
    If shape is 7 then,
        'gen pass a bad value to' of gen_number_flag and "99999999999999999999",
        Return a number, 0.
    (8: a flag whose value is missing because argv ends. Leaves the flag
     empty and does not raise.)
    If shape is 8 then,
        'gen pass a lone alias for' of gen_text_flag,
        Return a number, 0.
    (9: an unknown flag plus positionals. Consumes nothing, so both fall
     through to arguments's all - which has only ever seen the empty
     case. The alias is drawn from OUTSIDE the manifest's letters where
     it can be: an alias this program happens to declare is not unknown.)
    a text called 'the unknown alias' is 'gen an undeclared alias'.
    append 'the unknown alias' to gen_argv.
    a number called extra is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'the extra' is "extra{extra}".
    append 'the extra' to gen_argv.
    Return a number, 0.

(One alias for a flag, short or long. Both reach the same schema and the
 two are different code paths through the parser, so which one an
 invocation uses is drawn - and drawn through a prime modulus, because
 two consecutive small-range draws out of this LCG are anti-correlated
 (measured 2000 trials, 2000 disagreements) and this decision sits right
 next to two others.)

To 'gen alias for' with a number called 'the flag'.
    a number called 'the form draw' is 'rng below' of 13.
    If 'the form draw' is greater than 6 then,
        a text called 'the long alias' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_long,
        Return a text, 'the long alias'.
    a text called 'the short alias' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_short.
    Return a text, 'the short alias'.

(A valid invocation: pass whichever of the three flag types this program
 actually declared, and remember what each should hold so the assertions
 can check it. A program with no flags at all passes nothing and asserts
 nothing, which is a perfectly ordinary invocation of a program that
 takes no flags.)

To 'gen build a valid argv'.
    Set gen_asserts_argv to true.
    If gen_text_flag is not 0 then,
        a text called 'the label alias' is 'gen alias for' of gen_text_flag,
        a text called 'the label value' is 'gen a flag value',
        append 'the label alias' to gen_argv,
        append 'the label value' to gen_argv,
        Set gen_expected_label to 'the label value'.
    If gen_number_flag is not 0 then,
        a text called 'the count alias' is 'gen alias for' of gen_number_flag,
        (No ceiling. This used to be `'rng below' of 10000`, which the
         invariant report read back as "never above 9984" - a bound no
         line of LANGUAGE.md sets. Eighteen digits is the widest a
         literal can be and stay inside i64 by construction, and a
         number flag parses one: hand-verified against 0.4.7 with a
         fifteen-digit value.)
        a number called 'the digit count' is 'rng below' of 18 add 1,
        a text called 'the count value' is 'gen random digits' of 'the digit count',
        append 'the count alias' to gen_argv,
        append 'the count value' to gen_argv,
        Set gen_expected_count to 'the count value'.
    If gen_boolean_flag is not 0 then,
        a number called 'the boolean draw' is 'rng below' of 11,
        If 'the boolean draw' is less than 6 then,
            a text called 'the boolean alias' is 'gen alias for' of gen_boolean_flag,
            append 'the boolean alias' to gen_argv,
            Set gen_expects_on to true.
    Return a number, 0.

(A flag's text value. Any bytes would do - it is compared as text - so
 this is a drawn word plus drawn digits, which is wide enough that the
 assertion line's literal is never the same twice and narrow enough that
 nothing in it needs escaping in a Vox string or a shell-free argv.)

To 'gen a flag value'.
    a text called 'a word' is 'gen word from' of gen_type_words.
    a number called 'the digit count' is 'rng below' of 6 add 1.
    a text called 'the digits' is 'gen random digits' of 'the digit count'.
    Return a text, "{'a word'} {'the digits'}".

(The hostile shapes, aimed at a flag of the right type when there is
 one. A program with no number flag cannot be given a bad number, so it
 gets the undeclared-alias shape instead - the hostile case that needs
 nothing from the schema.)

To 'gen pass a bad value to' with a number called 'the flag' and a text called 'the value'.
    If 'the flag' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the unknown alias' is 'gen an undeclared alias',
        append 'the unknown alias' to gen_argv,
        append 'the value' to gen_argv,
        Return a number, 0.
    a text called 'the alias' is 'gen alias for' of 'the flag'.
    append 'the alias' to gen_argv.
    append 'the value' to gen_argv.
    Return a number, 0.

To 'gen pass a lone alias for' with a number called 'the flag'.
    If 'the flag' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the unknown alias' is 'gen an undeclared alias',
        append 'the unknown alias' to gen_argv,
        Return a number, 0.
    a text called 'the alias' is 'gen alias for' of 'the flag'.
    append 'the alias' to gen_argv.
    Return a number, 0.

(An alias no schema in this program claims. The letters a program uses
 are drawn from the alphabet on a coprime walk, so a scan for an unused
 one always terminates; if every letter were somehow spoken for the long
 form falls back to a spelling the dashed-alias rule can never produce,
 since a manifest phrase never contains a digit.)

To 'gen an undeclared alias'.
    (Marks first, then one scan. A scan nested inside a scan would put a
     While inside a While, and this generator's own rule (note 6b) is
     that a loop lives at its own level - so the two passes are two
     loops, not one inside the other.)
    a list called 'the letter is taken' is [].
    a number called 'the step' is 0.
    While 'the step' is less than 26,
        append 0 to 'the letter is taken',
        Set 'the step' to 'the step' add 1.
    a number called 'how many flags' is gen_flag_short's length.
    a number called 'the flag' is 1.
    While 'the flag' is less than or equal to 'how many flags',
        a text called 'the taken alias' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_short,
        a number called 'the taken slot' is 'gen letter slot of' of 'the taken alias',
        If 'the taken slot' is not 0 then,
            Set element 'the taken slot' of 'the letter is taken' to 1.
        Set 'the flag' to 'the flag' add 1.
    a number called 'the letter' is 0.
    a number called 'the scan' is 1.
    While 'the scan' is less than or equal to 26,
        a number called 'the mark' is element 'the scan' of 'the letter is taken',
        If 'the mark' is 0 and 'the letter' is 0 then,
            Set 'the letter' to 'the scan'.
        Set 'the scan' to 'the scan' add 1.
    If 'the letter' is 0 then,
        (Every letter is spoken for, which needs 26 flags in one
         program. A manifest phrase never contains a digit, so the
         dashed-alias rule can never produce this spelling and it is
         undeclared by construction.)
        Return a text, "--no-such-flag-1".
    a text called 'the free letter' is element 'the letter' of gen_alias_letters.
    Return a text, "-{'the free letter'}".

To 'gen letter slot of' with a text called alias.
    a number called 'the slot' is 0.
    a number called 'the step' is 1.
    While 'the step' is less than or equal to 26,
        a text called 'the letter' is element 'the step' of gen_alias_letters,
        a text called 'the candidate' is "-{'the letter'}",
        If alias is 'the candidate' then,
            Set 'the slot' to 'the step'.
        Set 'the step' to 'the step' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the slot'.

(The assertions. Only emitted for a valid invocation, and only for the
 flags actually passed. A distinct Exit code per assertion means a
 finding's exit status says WHICH expectation failed, without needing
 the output.)

To 'gen emit argv assertions'.
    If gen_asserts_argv is false then,
        Return a number, 0.
    (Only the flags this invocation actually supplied. A program whose
     manifest holds no number flag was passed no number and has nothing
     to check; a program with no flags at all asserts nothing, which is
     what a program that takes no flags should do.)
    If gen_text_flag is not 0 then,
        a text called 'the label name' is 'gen flag written' of gen_text_flag,
        'gen emit' of "If {'the label name'} is not \"{gen_expected_label}\" then, Exit 91.\n".
    If gen_number_flag is not 0 then,
        a text called 'the count name' is 'gen flag written' of gen_number_flag,
        'gen emit' of "If {'the count name'} is not {gen_expected_count} then, Exit 92.\n".
    If gen_boolean_flag is not 0 then,
        'gen emit boolean flag assertion'.
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    Return a number, 0.

(The boolean flag, both ways round. When the invocation passed it, it
 must be true; when it did not, it must hold its DECLARED DEFAULT -
 false when it has none (LANGUAGE.md:4465-4468), true when the manifest
 gave it `with default true`. Asserting `is false` unconditionally is
 what would break the moment a boolean flag drew a default, which is
 exactly the sort of assertion CLAUDE.md calls a false-finding factory.)

To 'gen emit boolean flag assertion'.
    a text called 'the switch name' is 'gen flag written' of gen_boolean_flag.
    If gen_expects_on then,
        'gen emit' of "If {'the switch name'} is false then, Exit 93.\n",
        Return a number, 0.
    a text called 'the default' is element gen_boolean_flag of gen_flag_default.
    If 'the default' is "true" then,
        'gen emit' of "If {'the switch name'} is false then, Exit 93.\n",
        Return a number, 0.
    'gen emit' of "If {'the switch name'} then, Exit 94.\n".
    Return a number, 0.

(Flags. Josj, 2026-08-20: "Flags don't appear anywhere." They did not,
 and the language's own flag schema went unexercised by anything real
 until vox-fuzz's CLI was rewritten onto it - which immediately produced
 compiler bugs #31 (a text flag with no default segfaulted on first
 read) and #32 (any flag read inside a function body was typed boolean
 whatever it was declared). A documented facility with no real user
 carries bugs indefinitely; this makes the fuzzer that user.

 NOTE this raises the fuzzer's floor to vox 0.4.6. On 0.4.5 and earlier
 an undefaulted text flag segfaults on read, so every generated program
 would "find" #31 - a true finding, but one already fixed and recorded.
 SEEDS.md pins the compiler commit, which is what keeps that honest.

 What the old block asserted without a citation, and what replaces it:

 - "every program has four flags": nothing says so. The count is drawn
   0..4 plus the scratch flag when this program does file I/O, and zero
   flags is a legal, ordinary Vox program.
 - "they are called fl<N>label / fl<N>count / fl<N>on / fl<N>scratch,
   with -a/-b/-c/-s and --alpha<N>/--beta<N>/--gamma<N>/--scratch<N>":
   nothing says so. Names and both aliases come off the manifest.
 - "one text, one number, one boolean, in that order": nothing says so.
   Each flag's type is drawn.
 - "only the text flag may carry a default": nothing says so.
   LANGUAGE.md:4465-4468 gives every type an empty value AND allows
   `with default` on every type - both hand-verified against 0.4.7,
   including `with default true` on a boolean.
 - "`Parse flags.` is always written": LANGUAGE.md:4478 says the
   opposite - omit it and Vox inserts parsing immediately after the last
   schema declaration. Verified: a program with two flags, a global
   between them and no `Parse flags.` compiles and reads both.
 - "the four declarations are contiguous": LANGUAGE.md:4484 explicitly
   permits normal code before and between schema declarations, and the
   compiler agrees.

 What genuinely holds, and is enforced here:

 - a schema must precede the parse point, and a flag read before the
   parse point is a compile-time error (LANGUAGE.md:4487). Every read
   this generator emits is either in the statement stream, which follows
   every block, or inside a function body, which is legal wherever the
   function is defined because the CALL happens after the parse point -
   verified, and the reason the reader may be emitted before the schema.
 - declaring a schema AFTER `Parse flags.` is a compile-time error
   (LANGUAGE.md:4486), so the parse line, when it is written at all,
   goes after every declaration in this block.
 - a `required` flag would abort at runtime whenever argv did not
   supply it, and the invariant is a program that compiles AND runs, so
   none is ever emitted.)

To 'gen emit prelude flags'.
    a number called 'how many flags' is gen_flag_names's length.
    a number called 'the flag' is 1.
    While 'the flag' is less than or equal to 'how many flags',
        'gen emit one flag' of 'the flag',
        (LANGUAGE.md:4484: "You may place normal code before/between
         schema declarations." So sometimes something else goes here -
         an ordinary global, the same declaration 'gen emit globals
         block' writes - and the flag block stops being a contiguous
         run of lines.)
        a number called 'the interleave draw' is 'rng below' of 13,
        If 'the interleave draw' is less than 4 then,
            a number called 'the fresh global' is 'gen new var'.
        Set 'the flag' to 'the flag' add 1.
    (LANGUAGE.md:4478: omit it and Vox inserts parsing immediately after
     the last schema declaration, so writing it is optional whether or
     not there are flags to parse. Hand-verified both edges against
     0.4.7: `Parse flags.` with no schema at all compiles and runs, and
     two flags read with no parse line compile and hold their values.)
    a number called 'the parse draw' is 'rng below' of 11.
    If 'the parse draw' is greater than 3 then,
        'gen emit' of "Parse flags.\n".
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    (Immediately after the parse point: a flag read before it is a
     compile-time error, and putting the assertions here means they run
     before anything else can Exit for its own reasons.)
    'gen emit argv assertions'.
    Return a number, 0.

To 'gen emit one flag' with a number called 'the flag'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen flag written' of 'the flag'.
    a text called 'the short alias' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_short.
    a text called 'the long alias' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_long.
    a number called 'the kind' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_kind.
    a text called 'the default' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_default.
    a text called 'the type' is "text".
    If 'the kind' is 1 then,
        Set 'the type' to "number".
    If 'the kind' is 2 then,
        Set 'the type' to "boolean".
    a text called 'the line' is "a flag called {'the name'} is \"{'the short alias'}\" or \"{'the long alias'}\", it is a {'the type'}.\n".
    If 'the default' is not "" then,
        Set 'the line' to "a flag called {'the name'} is \"{'the short alias'}\" or \"{'the long alias'}\", it is a {'the type'} with default {'the default'}.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the line'.
    Return a number, 0.

(The reader function is the #32 shape and the whole reason this is
 worth emitting: a flag read INSIDE a function body was mis-typed, and
 was invisible at top level because the declaration's own type is still
 in scope there. Reading each flag both ways is what makes the
 difference observable - so the reader reads EVERY flag this program
 declared, whatever their types, rather than the three the old template
 knew about.

 A program with no flags gets no reader: there would be nothing in its
 body, and a function body cannot be empty.)

To 'gen emit prelude flag reader'.
    If gen_uses_flag_reader is false then,
        Return a number, 0.
    a number called 'how many flags' is gen_flag_names's length.
    If 'how many flags' is 0 then,
        Return a number, 0.
    'gen emit' of "To {gen_reader_name}.\n".
    a number called 'the flag' is 1.
    While 'the flag' is less than or equal to 'how many flags',
        'gen emit one flag read' of 'the flag',
        Set 'the flag' to 'the flag' add 1.
    'gen emit' of "\n".
    Return a number, 0.

(A flag read inside a function body, in the shape bug #32 broke: bind it
 to a local of its declared type first, then print the local. A number
 also gets an arithmetic use, which is what made the mis-typing show.)

To 'gen emit one flag read' with a number called 'the flag'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen flag written' of 'the flag'.
    a number called 'the kind' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_kind.
    a text called 'the local' is 'gen reader local for' of 'the flag'.
    If 'the kind' is 0 then,
        'gen emit' of "    a text called {'the local'} is {'the name'}.\n",
        'gen emit' of "    Print {'the local'}.\n",
        Return a number, 0.
    If 'the kind' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the addend' is 'literal integer',
        'gen emit' of "    a number called {'the local'} is {'the name'} add {'the addend'}.\n",
        'gen emit' of "    Print {'the local'}.\n",
        Return a number, 0.
    'gen emit' of "    a boolean called {'the local'} is {'the name'}.\n".
    'gen emit' of "    Print {'the local'}.\n".
    Return a number, 0.

(A local named after the flag it copies, so the reader still reads
 aloud. It is a function local, so it may repeat a top-level name
 without colliding - but it never does, because the suffix is a word no
 flag phrase ends in.)

To 'gen reader local for' with a number called 'the flag'.
    a text called 'the phrase' is element 'the flag' of gen_flag_names.
    a text called 'the local phrase' is "{'the phrase'} as read".
    a text called 'the local' is 'gen written name' of 'the local phrase'.
    Return a text, 'the local'.

(Top-level flag reads, plus a call into the reader. Every flag is read
 at top level as well as inside the reader, which is what makes bug
 #32's difference observable. A program with no flags has nothing to
 read and stands aside.)

To 'gen leaf flag read' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'how many flags' is gen_flag_names's length.
    If 'how many flags' is 0 then,
        'gen manifest stand aside' of indent and nested,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a list called 'the lines' is [].
    a number called 'the flag' is 1.
    While 'the flag' is less than or equal to 'how many flags',
        a text called 'the name' is 'gen flag written' of 'the flag',
        a text called 'the print line' is "Print {'the name'}",
        append 'the print line' to 'the lines',
        Set 'the flag' to 'the flag' add 1.
    (The top-level reads always work; the CALL only goes in when this
     program actually defined the reader. Flags are still read either
     way, so coverage of the flag surface does not depend on the
     decision - only the function call does.)
    If gen_uses_flag_reader then,
        Set gen_reader_calls to gen_reader_calls add 1,
        append gen_reader_name to 'the lines'.
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.
    Return a boolean, false.

(An `is empty` probe on the flag and on plain values, in branch form.
 Every answer here is knowable at generation time, which is what makes
 this leaf a candidate for a real output oracle later; today it only
 proves the predicate compiles and does not crash.)

To 'gen leaf predicate probe' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a text called 'the number literal' is 'gen extreme number'.
    a text called 'blank decl' is "a text called pt{n} is \"\"".
    a text called 'blank probe' is "If pt{n} is empty then, Print \"pt{n} empty\". Otherwise, Print \"pt{n} filled\"".
    a text called 'number decl' is "a number called pn{n} is {'the number literal'}".
    a text called 'even probe' is "If pn{n} is even then, Print \"pn{n} even\". Otherwise, Print \"pn{n} odd\"".
    a text called 'sign probe' is "If pn{n} is positive then, Print \"pn{n} positive\". Otherwise, Print \"pn{n} nonpositive\"".
    a text called 'zero probe' is "If pn{n} is zero then, Print \"pn{n} zero\". Otherwise, Print \"pn{n} nonzero\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['blank decl', 'blank probe', 'number decl', 'even probe', 'sign probe', 'zero probe'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.
